"pandas, sqlalchemy, prophet, plotly\npandas\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Define the SQL query\nquery = \"\"\"\nSELECT Cle_Produit, Date_Facturation, SUM(Quantite) AS Total_Quantite \nFROM ventes \nGROUP BY Cle_Produit, Date_Facturation\n\"\"\"\n\n# Create a connection to the database\nengine = create_engine(\"mysql+pymysql://root:Password@localhost:3306/data\")\n\n# Read the query result\ndf = pd.read_sql_query(query, engine)\n\n# Pivot the data\ndf = df.pivot(index='Date_Facturation', columns='Cle_Produit', values='Total_Quantite')\n\n# Reset the index\ndf = df.reset_index()\n\n# Rename the columns\ndf.rename(columns={'Date_Facturation': 'ds', 'Total_Quantite': 'y'}, inplace=True)\n\n# Fit the Prophet model\nm = Prophet()\nm.fit(df)\n\n# Make predictions\nfuture = m.make_future_dataframe(periods=365)\nforecast = m.predict(future)\n\n# Save the forecast to a CSV file\nforecast[['ds', 'yhat']].to_csv('forecast.csv', index=False)"
"Here are some Python packages that may be helpful in solving the problem:\n\n- `pycuda`: This package provides a Python interface to the CUDA API, which is used by NVIDIA GPUs. It allows you to write CUDA code from Python and use it in your Python applications.\n- `cupy`: This package provides a Python interface to the CUDA API, similar to `pycuda`. It is designed to be faster and more efficient than `pycuda` and is optimized for use with the Numba compiler.\n- `torch.cuda`: This module provides a set of functions and classes for working with the CUDA API in PyTorch. It includes functions for managing the GPU, loading and saving models, and performing computations on the GPU.\n- `torch.cuda.test`: This module provides a set of tests for the CUDA API in PyTorch. It includes tests for various CUDA functions and classes.\n- `torch.cuda.test._test_cuda`: This module provides a set of tests for the CUDA API in PyTorch that are specific to the NVIDIA GPU.\n- `torch.cuda.test._test_cuda._test_cuda_utils`: This module provides a set of tests for the CUDA API in PyTorch that are specific to the NVIDIA GPU and use the `pycuda` package.\n- `pytorch.cuda`: This module provides a set of functions and classes for working with the CUDA API in PyTorch. It includes functions for managing the GPU, loading and saving models, and performing computations on the GPU.\n- `pytorch.cuda.test`: This module provides a set of tests for the CUDA API in PyTorch. It includes tests for various CUDA functions and classes.\n\nThese packages may help you to diagnose and solve the issue of not being able to find the CUDA drivers on your machine, as well as to use the GPU in your Python application."
