n from the database using the `delete()` method.\n\nHere's an example code snippet that demonstrates these steps:\n```python\nimport mysql.connector\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Connect to the MySQL database\ncnx = mysql.connector.connect(\n    user='username',\n    password='password',\n    host='localhost',\n    database='database_name'\n)\n\n# Retrieve the session data from the database\nsession_data = pd.read_sql_query(\"SELECT * FROM sessions\", cnx)\n\n# Parse the session data\nsession_data = session_data.set_index('session_id')\n\n# Get the user's session ID and created time\nsession_id = session_data.loc['user_id', 'created_time']\n\n# Set the user's session ID and created time\nsession_data.loc['user_id', 'session_id'] = session_id\nsession_data.loc['user_id', 'created_time'] = session_id\n\n# Get the user's session ID and created time again\nsession_id = session_data.loc['user_id', 'session_id']\ncreated_time = session_data.loc['user_id', 'created_time']\n\n# Check if the session ID has expired\nif session_id > pd.to_datetime('now') - pd.Timedelta(minutes=10):\n    # Delete the session from the database\n    cnx.cursor().execute(\"DELETE FROM sessions WHERE session_id = %s\", (session_id,))\n    cnx.commit()\n    print(\"Session deleted\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Session not expired\")\n```\nNote that this is just an example code snippet and may need to be modified to fit your specific use case. Additionally, you may need to handle errors and exceptions more robustly in a production environment."
