"ObservableCollection, INotifyPropertyChanged, and MVVM are all related concepts in .NET MAUI, but they're not directly related to the issue you're experiencing.\n\nHere are some suggestions that might help:\n\n1.  **Use a more robust data binding approach**: Instead of using `Binding` to bind to the `MyObvList` property, consider using a more robust data binding approach like `DataTemplate` or `ListView.ItemTemplate`. This can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n2.  **Use a `List<T>` instead of `ObservableCollection<T>`**: `ObservableCollection<T>` is a wrapper around a `List<T>`, and it's not guaranteed to work correctly in all scenarios. Consider using a `List<T>` directly.\n\n3.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `IList<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `IList<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n4.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyCollectionChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyCollectionChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n5.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n6.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n7.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n8.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n9.  **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\n10. **Use a `List<T>` with a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` implementation**: You can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class, which can help ensure that the data is being updated correctly.\n\nHere's an example of how you can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class:\n\n```csharp\npublic interface INotifyPropertyChanged<T> : INotifyPropertyChanged\n{\n    event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;\n    void OnPropertyChanged(string propertyName);\n}\n```\n\nAnd here's an example of how you can implement a custom `INotifyPropertyChanged<T>` interface for your `MyData` class:\n\n```csharp\npublic class MyData : INotifyPropertyChanged\n{\n    private string number { get; set; }\n    public string Number\n    {\n        get { return number; }\n        set\n        {\n            if (number != value)\n            {\n                number = value;\n                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Number));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private string name { get; set; }\n    public string Name\n    {\n        get { return name; }\n        set\n        {\n            if (name != value)\n            {\n                name = value;\n                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Name));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    private string info { get; set; }\n    public string Info\n    {\n        get { return info; } \n        set { \n            if (info != value)  { \n                info = value; \n                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Info)); \n            } \n        }\n    }\n\n    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;\n    protected virtual void OnPropertyChanged(string propertyName)\n    {\n        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nYou can then use this interface in your `MyData` class:\n\n```csharp\npublic class MyData : INotifyPropertyChanged\n{\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAnd you can use the `INotifyPropertyChanged` interface in your `MyObvList` class:\n\n```csharp\npublic class MyObvList : ObservableCollection<MyData>\n{\n    // ...\n}\n```"
"ControlTemplate, ContentPresenter, and BindingContext\n-----------------------------------------------\n\nThe issue you're facing is due to the fact that the `ContentPresenter` in the `CardView` control is not using the binding context of the page it's part of. This is because the `ContentPresenter` is not being set to the correct `BindingSource` in its `ControlTemplate`.\n\nTo fix this, you need to set the `ContentPresenter` to the correct `BindingSource` in its `ControlTemplate`. You can do this by adding the following code to the `ControlTemplate` of the `CardView` control:\n\n```xaml\n&lt;ContentPresenter Content=&quot;{TemplateBinding Header}&quot;/&gt;\n```\n\nThis will set the `ContentPresenter` to the correct binding source, which is the page it's part of.\n\nAdditionally, you need to set the `ContentPresenter` to the correct `BindingSource` in the `CardView` control's `OnInitialized` method:\n\n```csharp\nprotected override void OnInitialized(EventArgs e)\n{\n    base.OnInitialized(e);\n    ContentPresenter = new ContentPresenter\n    {\n        Content = Header,\n        ContentTemplate = new ControlTemplate\n        {\n            TargetProperty = ContentPresenter.ContentProperty,\n            ChildContentTemplate = new ControlTemplate\n            {\n                TargetProperty = ContentPresenter.ContentTemplateProperty,\n                ChildContentTemplate = new ContentTemplate\n                {\n                    ChildContentTemplateProperty = new ContentTemplateProperty\n                    {\n                        BindingContext = this\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    };\n}\n```\n\nThis will set the `ContentPresenter` to the correct binding source, which is the page it's part of.\n\nStyle and Binding\n----------------\n\nYou also need to set the `ControlTemplate` of the `CardView` control to use the correct `Style` and `BindingContext`:\n\n```xaml\n&lt;Style TargetType=&quot;local:CardView&quot;&gt;\n